text:
1.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you reached the Terminal in RStudio Server and know it is not the Console;
session_01_commands.sh is saved, includes the commands that failed, and runs from an empty folder;
pwd ends in bioinfo_course/session_01/data, and that folder contains mystery.fasta;
you can say the file has 40 records, that grep -c "^&gt;" is the reliable way to count them, and why wc -l gives a different number;
you can name the record that is not DNA and the record that was truncated, and say how you spotted each;
you can explain in one sentence that a FASTA record starts on a line whose first character is &gt;.

2.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can name the four lines of a FASTQ record and say what line 4 encodes;
you can read a !/#/,/:/F/I character as roughly bad-to-excellent without recomputing the formula;
../results holds three FastQC reports, produced by a fastqc command you ran yourself;
you can point to one plot in a report and say, in one sentence, why it does or does not worry you;
you made a trust/flag decision on an unseen sample, backed by two named observations.

3.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can say what pident, evalue, and bitscore each roughly mean, and can name a real case from today where they did not all agree;
you ran a strict and a sensitive BLAST search and can explain why they gave different numbers of hits;
you built a tree yourself with FastTree and can say what its support values roughly mean;
you can explain, in your own words, how an alignment turns into a tree, and read a distance matrix as a table;
you fetched real sequences from NCBI yourself, built a 114-tip tree from them (including unknown_sample), and can say where the named variants sit relative to Wuhan-Hu-1 and to each other;
you can say where unknown_sample sits in that tree, and why that is independent confirmation of blocks B-D2’s conclusion;
you re-rooted that tree on Wuhan-Hu-1 and can explain both why the named variants’ groupings did not change, and why that reroot gave a less clean picture than rooting on unknown_sample;
you compared a nucleotide-level and a protein-level percent identity for the same gene pair;
you have a complete draft interpretation in WORKSHEET.md, ready to polish and submit by Thursday.

4.5 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

your session_04 folder is an R project with data/, results/ and a workbook that renders;
you can explain the design of the experiment from your map: the conditions, why they are mixed in space, and why each plant had one job;
you can say how much drought and the heatwave reduced the plants’ biomass, and which organ suffered most;
you can say what a row and a column of alpha represent, and why some compartments have fewer than 40 samples;
you used filter, mutate, count, group_by + summarise and pivot_longer, and can say in one sentence what each one did;
you built a ggplot with boxes, points, colours and facets, a map with geom_tile(), and an interaction plot with means, lines and error bars;
you used a Tukey test to find which compartments differ, and know which test to pick for a new question from the table;
you ran a t-test and found the same p-value with lm();
you fitted lm(shannon ~ water * heat * genotype) on two compartments and can say, for each, which factor matters and how much of the variation it explains;
you wrote a p-value on a plot from a value computed in R, not typed by hand;
you inserted a saved figure into your report, with a caption, and cited it with @fig-bulk;
you can explain why a significant effect is not necessarily a large one.

5.5 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can explain why concentrations are log-transformed and scaled before a PCA;
you turned a long table into a matrix with pivot_wider();
you can read a PCA: the share of variance, the scores and the loadings;
you can read a heatmap with clustering, and say what its trees are computed from;
you can explain why a PLS-DA that separates groups proves nothing until it is cross-validated, with the numbers of D2 and D3;
you can read a volcano plot, and say why the p-values were adjusted;
you can compare the leaf with the root, and say what is different;
you can say what targeted and untargeted metabolomics each see, and why a putative name from an untargeted search is not an identification;
you can say what the heatwave does to the exudates, and why it matters for the soil bacteria of Session 4.

6.5 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can say what a SNP is and what 0 and 1 mean in the genotype table;
you tested one SNP with lm(), then all of them with a function and apply();
you can explain the Bonferroni threshold, and read a Manhattan plot;
you can read a QQ plot and λ, and say when a scan cannot be trusted;
you can explain, with the SNP of C3, how population structure creates false associations;
you ran a PCA of the genotypes, and used its components to correct the GWAS;
you found the genes under a peak, and can say why a peak is not a proof;
you can explain why the structure misleads the GWAS of flowering time much more than that of leaf sodium.

7.10 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can explain, with the chihuahua and the muffin, why machine learning exists;
you can place AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI in each other;
you trained a decision tree and read its questions;
you can read a confusion matrix and compute an accuracy, a recall and a precision;
you can explain overfitting, and why a model is judged on a test set;
you can explain why a forest of trees votes better than one tree;
you can say what a neuron, a weight, a loss and an epoch are;
you computed a convolution, and can say what the filters of a CNN learn;
you can explain why an accuracy of 87% can hide a poor model for rare species;
you can explain how an alignment and coevolution let AlphaFold predict a structure, and judge a prediction with its pLDDT and PAE;
you used four AI tools for biology, and checked the answer of a chatbot.
